\section{Playing back lectures}
\label{tut:playing-lectures}

A lecture's live coding, recorded with learnlog and exported as a
bundle, is a log like the learner's own, and this tutorial is about
reading someone else's log from inside one's own project.
The learner already knows playback, tagging and checkout from
\cref{tut:playback-and-tagging}; what varies here is the
\emph{source}---a file they were given instead of their project---and
the invariants are the commands, which are the same with [[-f]] or
[[--from]] added.
That is the tutorial's first object of learning: a shared log is
played and used with the tools the learner already has.
The second is that their own log keeps track of the reading: what
they watched, the code they took from it, what they did with that
code, and where they stopped, so that continuing needs no notes and
a teacher can later see the lecture beside the work it led to.

The steps follow the student's evening: get the lecture into the
project; play it; find the parts the lecturer named; take the code of
one moment of it; run and change that code; find those runs again by file; pick the lecture up again
where it stopped; and finally take the copied code out again.
The middle steps are where the reading turns into writing---the
copied code is run, edited and looked up like any program---and the
tutorial puts them between the two playback steps on purpose, so that
the continue step demonstrates a bookmark that survived the learner's
own work, not one used a second later.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

As in \cref{tut:playback-and-tagging}, the tutorial tangles into a
novice entry, [[playing-lectures]], and an expert entry,
[[playing-lectures-expert]], sharing every step's checks through
common chunks.
The expert extras name what the log records at each step and the
rules behind the defaults: that the bundle file itself is recorded,
that a copy overlays where a checkout replaces, that the bookmark is
the learner's own, how a tag expression and a file pattern are read,
and that an undo is one more checkout.

