\subsection{Put the lecture in your project}

The opening step is about place, and it exists because the rest of
the tutorial depends on it: a bundle inside the project is one the
log can record, a bundle in a downloads folder is not.
A learner working through the tutorial without a teacher's bundle
makes one from their own log, which is the same kind of file; the
check accepts either the export or a listing that shows the file is
there.
The expert extra says why the place matters, in the log's terms.

<<shared step: put the lecture in your project>>=
# Put the lecture in your project

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: '(?:learnlog\s+export\b[^\n]*\S+\.learnlog\b|ls\b[^\n]*)'
hint: Copy the `.learnlog` file into the project folder, or make one with `learnlog export -o lecture.learnlog`, then `ls`.
```

Your teacher gives you a lecture's live coding as a file ending in
`.learnlog`.
Put it in your project folder, the one you run `learnlog` in, and run
`ls` to see it there.
If you have no lecture file to hand, make one from your own log with
`learnlog export -o lecture.learnlog`; it works the same way.
<<novice step: put the lecture in your project>>=
<<shared step: put the lecture in your project>>
<<expert step: put the lecture in your project>>=
<<shared step: put the lecture in your project>>

The place matters: the steps that follow record the lecture file in
your log along with what you do with it, and only a file inside the
project can be in a snapshot.
A file elsewhere still plays, but your log will only say that you
played it, not what.
@

\subsection{Play the lecture}

The playback step varies one thing against the viewer step of
\cref{tut:playback-and-tagging}: the source.
Its object of learning is that a bundle is playable as it is, with
the same viewer and the same keys, and nothing to unpack.
The learner is told to stop partway on purpose---the continue step
later needs a place to come back to---and to note a moment they want
the code of, which the next step uses.
The expert extra is what the log records: a review run in the
learner's own log, with the lecture file in its snapshot.

<<shared step: play the lecture>>=
# Play the lecture

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-f|--from)\s+\S+\.learnlog\b'
hint: Run `learnlog play -f lecture.learnlog`, watch a while, and quit with `q`.
```

Run `learnlog play -f lecture.learnlog` (with your file's name) to
open the lecture in the viewer instead of your own log.
Step through it with the arrow keys.
Stop somewhere in the middle and quit with `q`; before you do, find a
moment whose code you would like to have.
Its card ends with the command that copies that code into your
project, under `To copy this version into lecture/:`.
Note that line: `learnlog checkout -f lecture.learnlog` followed by the
moment's hash.
<<novice step: play the lecture>>=
<<shared step: play the lecture>>
<<expert step: play the lecture>>=
<<shared step: play the lecture>>

The playback is a review run in your own log, hidden from `learnlog
list` until you ask with `--all`, and its record names every moment
you looked at.
The lecture file goes into that run's snapshot, even though
`.learnlog` files are otherwise kept out of every snapshot so that a
log never contains its own exports: a teacher reading your log later
can open the lecture you watched.
@

\subsection{Find the lecture's parts}

A lecturer who tags each part of the lecture as they start it leaves
names in the bundle, and this step is about finding them.
Its object of learning is that a tag is a named moment, usable
wherever a hash is, and that [[--tags]] turns the listing of a whole
lecture into a table of contents.
The source stays the same while the flag varies:
[[learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog]] first, every run of the
lecture, then the same command with [[--tags]], only the tagged ones.
What the flag removes is the part of the listing the learner does not
need in order to find their way.
The step then uses one tag as a start point for [[play]], so the name
is seen doing a hash's job before the next step asks for one.

It sits after the first playback, where the learner noted a moment by
the command on its card, hash included, so that a tag arrives as a
name for the same kind of moment.
It sits before the copy, so that the copy step can take a tag as well
as a hash.
A learner whose stand-in bundle is their own export may have no tags
at all.
The listing then says [[No tagged runs.]], and the text prepares them
for that rather than making the check depend on tags existing.
The expert extra gives the expression rules and the start-marker
reading of a tag.

<<shared step: find the lecture's parts>>=
# Find the lecture's parts

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+list\b(?=[^\n]*(?:-f|--from)\s+\S+\.learnlog\b)[^\n]*--tags\b'
hint: Run `learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog --tags`, with your file's name.
```

Run `learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog`: it lists every run of the
lecture, the way `learnlog list` lists yours.
Now run it again with `--tags` added,
`learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog --tags`: only the runs your teacher
tagged are left, each with its tag at the end of the line, for
example `(tag: lecture-loops)`.
A tag marks where a part of the lecture starts.
You can type the tag wherever you would type a hash:
`learnlog play -f lecture.learnlog lecture-loops` opens the lecture at
that part.
If your file is an export of your own log, it may say
`No tagged runs.`; a lecture file usually has a tag for each part.
<<novice step: find the lecture's parts>>=
<<shared step: find the lecture's parts>>
<<expert step: find the lecture's parts>>=
<<shared step: find the lecture's parts>>

After `--tags` you can give a regular expression, and only the tags it
matches anywhere in their name are listed: `--tags loops` keeps the
tags with `loops` in them, and `--tags '^lecture21-'` the ones that
start that way.
A tag names its own run and every run after it, until the next tag,
so tagging each part as it starts splits the lecture into parts.
Tags on the lecturer's own `learnlog list` or `learnlog play` runs are
listed too, although `learnlog list` otherwise hides those runs.
@

\subsection{Take the code of one moment}

This step turns reading into having.
Its object of learning is that a moment of someone else's log can
become files in your project, that the copy adds rather than
replaces---a folder named after the lecture, beside your own
files---and that it is recorded as a checkout, which the learner
sees in [[learnlog list]].
The contrast with the checkout the learner knows is the source,
[[--from]], and the consequence is the folder.
The expert extra names the folder rule and [[-o]], and says why a
copy from another log overlays where a checkout in your own log
replaces.

The learner does not have to compose the command.
Every card of another log ends with it, source, hash and destination
folder filled in (\cref{sec:way-back}), so the playback step tells
the learner to note that line rather than a hash, and this step gives
the same line back with the hash left as the one blank to fill.
The reading view's header shows no hash at all, only the position and
the file that ran; the card's last line is where a hash can be copied
from.
Handing over the line costs the step nothing it is about: the object
of learning is what the command does---the copy, the folder, the
record---not how to spell it, and a learner who has to reconstruct
[[--from]] and the file name from memory is practising recall of
syntax at the moment they should be noticing where the files land.

<<shared step: take the code of one moment>>=
# Take the code of one moment

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+checkout\b[^\n]*(?:-f|--from)\s+\S+\.learnlog\b\s+\S+'
  - next run continues
hint: Run the line you noted from the card, `learnlog checkout -f lecture.learnlog <hash>`, or use a tag instead of the hash.
```

Run the command you noted from the card,
`learnlog checkout -f lecture.learnlog <hash>`, with the moment's hash
(`-f` is short for `--from`).
One of the lecture's tags works instead of the hash.
The lecture's files at that moment land in a folder named after the
lecture file, `lecture/` for `lecture.learnlog`, and your own files
stay as they are.
Run `learnlog list`: the copy is in your log as a checkout, with the
lecture's name on its line.
<<novice step: take the code of one moment>>=
<<shared step: take the code of one moment>>
<<expert step: take the code of one moment>>=
<<shared step: take the code of one moment>>

The folder is the lecture file's name without its suffix; `-o` picks
another folder inside your project, and `-o .` is the project root,
where files of the same name are overwritten and the rest stay.
A checkout within your own log replaces your files, because its target
is an earlier state of this very project; a copy from another log
overlays, because that log's files are not yours to lose.
Either way the record starts with a snapshot of your files, and the
run you record next diffs against the copied code.
@

\subsection{Run and change the code}

The step that makes the tutorial more than a viewing exercise: the
copied program is run from its folder, changed, and run again, and
the learner sees in [[learnlog list]] that these runs are recorded
like any other.
Its object of learning is that code taken from a lecture is now
theirs to work on, with the log following as usual.
It sits between the two playback steps so that the bookmark the next
step relies on has survived real work.
The expert extra is what the record shows: the first run's diff is
against the lecture's code, so the log tells the learner's edits from
the lecturer's.

<<shared step: run and change the code>>=
# Run and change the code

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'python3?\s+\S+/\S+\.py\b'
hint: Run the copied program from its folder, e.g. `python lecture/main.py`, then change it and run it again.
```

Run the copied program from its folder, for example
`python lecture/main.py` (use the name of the file you got).
Change something in it and run it again.
Run `learnlog list`: both runs are in your log, like any run of your
own.
<<novice step: run and change the code>>=
<<shared step: run and change the code>>
<<expert step: run and change the code>>=
<<shared step: run and change the code>>

The copy closed its record with the copied files as the log's newest
version, so your first run of the program shows no diff of the
lecturer's code as yours, and your second run's diff is exactly your
change.
@

\subsection{Find the runs of one file}

By now the learner's log holds the lecture's playback, the copy, and
their own runs, and [[learnlog list]] shows them all.
This step asks the log about one file, and its object of learning is
that \enquote{ran it} and \enquote{changed it} are different
questions with different answers.
The file stays the same while the option varies from [[--runs]] to
[[--changes]], and the two listings differ where the two questions
do.
The first run of the copy ran the file without changing it, so it is
under [[--runs]] only.
The copy itself changed the file without running it, so it is under
[[--changes]] only.
The edited run is under both.
The last sentence varies the pattern instead, from
[[lecture/main.py]] to [[main.py]], so that the learner meets the
folder as the part of a pattern that makes it one file among
namesakes.
The expert extra gives the pattern rules and what \enquote{changed}
is measured against.

<<shared step: find the runs of one file>>=
# Find the runs of one file

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+list\b[^\n]*--(?:runs|changes)\b'
hint: Run `learnlog list --runs lecture/main.py`, then `learnlog list --changes lecture/main.py`, with your file's name.
```

Run `learnlog list --runs lecture/main.py` (with the name of the
file you ran): only your two runs of the copied program are listed.
Then run `learnlog list --changes lecture/main.py`: now the list is
the moments the file changed, the copy and your second run, but not
your first run, which ran the file without changing it.
Without the folder, `--runs main.py` lists the runs of every
`main.py`, in any folder of your project.
<<novice step: find the runs of one file>>=
<<shared step: find the runs of one file>>
<<expert step: find the runs of one file>>=
<<shared step: find the runs of one file>>

A pattern is read like a line in `.gitignore`, always from the project
root, whatever folder you type the command in.
A bare name matches in any folder, and a folder's name matches
everything in it; a path with a slash matches from the root, and
`./main.py` is only the one in the root.
`*` and `?` match within one folder level, so `lecture/*.py` is the
lecture's programs.
Repeat an option to ask for any of several files, and give both
options to ask for runs that match both.
Changed means changed since the run before, what `learnlog play`
shows under Code changes; a checkout changes the files it brings in.
@

\subsection{Pick the lecture up again}

The step re-uses [[--continue]] from \cref{tut:playback-and-tagging}
with one thing varied: no source is named, and the lecture opens
anyway, where the learner stopped.
The object of learning is that the bookmark is the learner's own---it
lives in their log, together with what was played---so continuing
needs neither the hash nor the file name.
The expert extra is the rule behind it.

<<shared step: pick the lecture up again>>=
# Pick the lecture up again

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-c\b|--continue\b)'
hint: Run `learnlog play -c` with no `-f`; it continues the lecture.
```

Run `learnlog play -c`, without naming the lecture file.
The viewer opens the lecture again, on the moment you were looking at
when you quit, and says so: your log remembers what you played as
well as where you stopped.
Watch some more and quit with `q`.
<<novice step: pick the lecture up again>>=
<<shared step: pick the lecture up again>>
<<expert step: pick the lecture up again>>=
<<shared step: pick the lecture up again>>

`--continue` looks in your own log for the newest playback and takes
its source along; with `-f` it only considers playbacks of that file.
The lecture's own review runs, if the lecturer made any, are never
your bookmark, and a playback that was killed before it finished left
no bookmark at all.
@

\subsection{Take the copied code out again}

The closing step takes the copied folder out again, and its object of
learning is that a checkout is undone by name, from a list, without a
hash.
[[--restore]] lists the log's checkouts newest first, the copy is the
first of them, and answering [[1]] brings back the files from before
it: the folder goes, the lecture file and the learner's own files
stay, and the edited copy is not lost---it is in the log.
The expert extra says what a restore is in the log: one more
checkout, appended, with the undone one still there.

<<shared step: take the copied code out again>>=
# Take the copied code out again

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+checkout\b[^\n]*--restore\b'
  - next run continues
hint: Run `learnlog checkout --restore` and answer `1`.
```

Run `learnlog checkout --restore`: it lists your checkouts, newest
first, and asks which one to undo.
Answer `1`, the copy you made.
The folder is gone, your own files and the lecture file are still
there, and the changes you made to the copy are in your log, ready to
be checked out again.
<<novice step: take the copied code out again>>=
<<shared step: take the copied code out again>>
<<expert step: take the copied code out again>>=
<<shared step: take the copied code out again>>

Every checkout records the files from before it as its own header, so
undoing one is checking that header out, and the list saves you the
hash.
A restore is itself a checkout, appended to the log like any other,
and the one it undoes stays in the history---which is why your edits
are still there.
@
